In repect to the computer science field:

What the AI does? 
AI allows generation of code that developers can use to save time on their projects. It 
can not be a complete replacement for coding or creating any applications, but it is good for 
simple tasks or to use as the foundation for projects. It requires a developer to still understand what the AI
is doing as it can often make mistakes that need to be monitored and fixed. It can be compared to someone 
trying to do thousands of simple addition problems by hand which might take a week, while the AI could generate 
it within seconds. Along the same line, the AI will probably make mistakes on high level math or theoretical 
concepts, meaning a human still must be there using AI as a supplement.

What data it uses?
AI's typically pull all data from the entire internet, however there may be some specific models that
are specialized into certain subjects and only look for keywords that reference their subject so they can 
pull relevant data. Also, some AI models use only data inputted from the user to predict or analyze what 
the user needs from it.

What business problems it solves?
The largest issue AI solves is optimization and speed in businesses. Similar to what was previously stated, it
reduces the simplest tasks into an almost instantaneous process. It allows businesses to focus on creativity and 
innovation rather than doing the same things repeatedly. Businesses will be more incentivized to make better 
products/applications and it may be easier to make profits. 

Who benefits?
Businesses save or make more money utilizing AI so that they can produce new and improved things, although this is 
provided they use AI properly as a supplement instead of a replacement for employees. Employees can benefit by 
offloading work to AI and then simply debugging code, therefore cutting down the time for projects significantly.
The businesses may not need to hire as many low level employees, and people with high creativity and critical thinking 
will be valued higher. Also with better optimization from businesses, consumers receive better products and higher quality
of life. 

What could go wrong?
THe problem many businesses face is the belief that they can save money by replacing all employees with AI coders. Considering
how human-like AI is now with LLM's, people fall under the assumption that it always understands what you want and knows how to
do everything. The problem with this is AI's can only operate under data given to them and that data is not always accurate either.
So it may seem like a good idea on the surface, but as soon as the AI starts making mistakes and there are not employees that 
understand what the AI is trying to accomplish, projects start to fall apart and deadlines are not being met. In the same vein, an employee 
might offload all of their work to AI without checking it to the point where they do not even understand the work they are submitting, and will 
ultimately be fired since they can not perform their job properly.
